#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078202 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01f44f8b-5db2-567a-89ce-9283f2f1b0c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of a Geometric Sequence Given the First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "f6a49cd7-9558-524c-b511-b13bd792cfc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-1509" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $2$, and the common ratio is $3$. What is the $9$th term?" :question/worked-solution "The first term is $a_1=2$, and the common ratio is $r=3$, so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}9}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} \\\\\na_{9} &= 2 \\cdot 3^{9 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{8} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 6561 \\\\\n&= 13122\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is\n\n$$\na_{9}=13\\,122\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d82eb202-fec9-5769-a006-67d42b932303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "f7b26998-6137-54f9-bfe1-a2c3ba5eb0f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1510" :question/problem "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $9$, and the common ratio is $3$, find the second term." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=3$, so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1$. We know that the $5$th term is $a_5=9$, so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{5} &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{5 - 1} \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{4} \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} \\cdot 81 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{81} &= a_{1} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{9} &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nwe can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second term is\n\n$$\na_2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078204 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5760a53a-1a0c-568e-ac9c-c820eaad3010" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Formula for the Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "4bf89b8f-f713-51b5-8536-5aa5b08f16d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1511" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $6$th term is $80$ and the common ratio is $-2$." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-2$, so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1$. We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80$, so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{6} &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{6 - 1} \\\\\n80 &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\\n80 &= a_{1} \\cdot (-32) \\\\\n- \\frac{80}{32} &= a_{1} \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{2} &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nwe can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-2)^{-1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-2)^{n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
